33:
"For phenomena (dhammā) that have arisen, their breakup is right before them;
Phenomena that are subject to dissolution stand without mixing with previous ones.

They come from the unmanifest; through breakup, they go to the manifest;
Like a flash of lightning in the sky, they arise and pass away."

Thus, life is brief because of the shortness of its duration.

How is life brief because of the shortness of its own nature? Life is bound up with the in-breath; life is bound up with the out-breath; life is bound up with both in-breath and out-breath. Life is bound up with the great elements (mahābhūta); life is bound up with nutriment (āhāra); life is bound up with heat (usma); life is bound up with consciousness (viññāṇa). The root of these, the karma-born body, is weak; the prior causes of these—ignorance, craving, and karma—are also weak. The conditions are weak; the phenomena currently arising are also weak. The co-existent mind and body are weak; the associated mental phenomena are weak; the conascent phenomena are weak; the phenomenon that joins death and rebirth is also weak. These phenomena are always mutually weak; they are mutually unstable. These phenomena cast each other down; they cannot protect each other; nor can they sustain each other. There is no one who makes them arise.

"No one is diminished by the power of anyone else;
These are entirely like Gandharvas.
They are produced by previous causes; even those that produce them died before;
Neither the previous nor the subsequent ever saw each other."

Thus, life is brief because of the shortness of its own nature.

Furthermore, in comparison to the life of the Cātumahārājika devas, the life of humans is brief, limited, short, momentary, fleeting, insignificant, not lasting, and of short duration. In comparison to the life of the Tāvatiṃsa devas... the Yāma devas... the Tusita devas... the Nimmānarati devas... the Paranimmitavasavatti devas... the Brahmakāyika devas, the life of humans is brief, limited, short, momentary, fleeting, insignificant, not lasting, and of short duration.

For this was said by the Blessed One:
